%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{abstract}
Urban disaster response unfolds as a coupled social process: residents receive
unequal information, negotiate within households, revise plans after
disruption, and act under shared physical constraints. Generative agents
produce linguistically plausible behavior, but social simulation requires
behavior that also holds under informational, social, and physical
constraints: what a resident believes, what a household has committed to, and
what the world permits are three different states. We present
\emph{DisasterSociety}, a generative agent-based simulation framework built on
this separation. A resident language model observes, remembers, plans, and
communicates, producing structured intentions; a household layer validates
accepted proposals into versioned commitments; and a deterministic world
arbitrates all intentions against one authoritative snapshot, returning
execution outcomes as feedback to the next round of cognition. In a
Carr-Fire-informed controlled scenario, a paired twelve-seed ablation
separates mechanism function from downstream necessity: memory, feedback,
planning, and interaction each operate at their own interfaces, yet only
feedback and interaction carry determined downstream effects---removing
feedback collapses complete safe household departure from 0.812 to 0.021, and
removing interaction eliminates coordinated departure. A deterministic
reference policy, a bounded seven-model replication, and instruction
paraphrases corroborate these two mechanism effects, and process studies at
24 and 100 households produce complete multi-step coordination and departure
processes with zero constraint violations, with level gaps against survey
references reported explicitly. DisasterSociety offers an inspectable framework for studying how
heterogeneous resident decisions become household and collective disaster
response.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Generative agents, multi-agent simulation, urban disaster response, household
coordination, social simulation, event-driven simulation, mechanism ablation.
\end{IEEEkeywords}


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}

Urban disaster response is not an isolated choice made at the instant a warning
arrives. Protective-action research distinguishes message receipt and
comprehension from threat appraisal, action appraisal, and the practical
impediments that shape whether and when a resident acts
\cite{lindell2012padm,mileti1992warnings,huang2012ike}. Wildfire studies likewise
show that warning exposure, preparation, and departure unfold over time rather
than collapsing into a single binary outcome
\cite{kuligowski2021wildfire,grajdura2021awareness}. The process is also social.
Household members may begin in different locations, coordinate gathering and
shared-vehicle use, decide whether to reunite, and depart together or in separate
groups \cite{liu2014uniting,maghelal2017split}.
The controlled running example in \cref{fig:running-example} follows one
household across these boundaries. Resident A receives an official warning
while Resident B has not, so co-residence does not synchronize their private
cognition even though they share a vehicle and a care obligation. Their message
exchange specifies a driver, companions, route, and departure time, and the
validated agreement becomes a household commitment. When the primary route
closes, world arbitration rejects the original plan; execution feedback then
supports a revised backup-route intention before the world permits departure. A
useful simulation must preserve every distinction in this chain: who observed
what, which proposal was merely sent, what the household actually committed to,
and which action the physical world ultimately executed.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/fig1_running_example.pdf}
  \caption{Running example of a coupled household evacuation process. (A)
  Asymmetric warning delivery gives co-resident adults different private beliefs
  while the household still shares a vehicle and care responsibility. (B) Message
  exchange converges to a validated household commitment that records route,
  vehicle, companions, and departure time. (C) Closure of the primary route R1
  causes world arbitration to reject the original plan; execution feedback
  triggers replanning to the open backup route R2, after which the revised action
  passes constraint checks and executes. The example is Carr-informed and
  controlled; it illustrates the modeled mechanisms rather than reconstructing
  the historical Carr Fire timeline.}
  \label{fig:running-example}
\end{figure*}

Agent-based models provide a natural bottom-up representation of heterogeneous
actors and their interactions, while large language models (LLMs) make it
possible to express context-sensitive judgments and communication without
enumerating every behavioral rule. Generative Agents connected memory,
reflection, planning, and interaction in a persistent artificial community
\cite{park2023generativeagents}; subsequent systems have scaled societal and
urban simulation with LLM agents, in some cases through hybrid
LLM/numerical-agent designs,
\cite{zhang2025agentsociety,bougie2025citysim,zhou2026gasim}. Disaster-oriented
work now spans theory-informed individual evacuation prediction
\cite{chen2025flare}, LLM residents embedded in spatial fire or crowd simulators
\cite{dang2025fireagents,yang2026whenagents}, and integrated hazard--population
demonstrations \cite{sultimov2026respond}. Each line captures a subset of the
process. Survey-based prediction estimates a one-shot individual evacuation
label but does not simulate the multi-step social process that produces it
\cite{chen2025flare}. Spatially embedded LLM residents react to fire or crowd
dynamics with communication and context-aware reasoning, yet communication
remains a natural-language exchange rather than a recipient-specific life
cycle that must terminate in a validated household commitment, and household
resource allocation stays implicit \cite{dang2025fireagents,yang2026whenagents}. Integrated demonstrations
couple hazards, alerts, and populations end to end, but do not isolate which
resident-level mechanisms produce the collective outcome
\cite{sultimov2026respond}. General-purpose social simulators, in turn, rarely
place an authoritative physical world behind agent communication
\cite{park2023generativeagents,zhang2025agentsociety}.

We argue that these omissions reflect one underlying modeling gap: three kinds
of state that disaster response keeps apart are conflated in a single
generative loop. A dynamic world is not the same object as a resident's belief
about it---co-residence cannot automatically synchronize inboxes, memories,
route beliefs, or unexpressed plans. A household is neither an indivisible
actor nor a shared mind---sending, delivering, or accepting words does not
itself reserve a vehicle, satisfy a care dependency, or make a departure time
feasible. And a linguistically plausible action is not necessarily an
executable action---contemporaneous intentions must be resolved against one
authoritative snapshot so that closed roads, vehicle capacity, member position,
and competing resource claims have consistent consequences. A resident-society
simulation that respects these constraints must make the three boundaries
explicit in its state model, not merely in its prose.

The research question of this paper follows directly: \emph{how can
LLM-driven residents form information-bounded, traceable, and executable
social behavior processes in a dynamic, constrained socio-physical
environment?} Information boundedness requires that private cognition contain
only information available through the resident's permitted observation,
delivery, and feedback channels; traceability requires an explicit
coordination record between a message and a shared plan; executability
requires that no agent output, however plausible, can mutate shared resources
or physical state without deterministic validation.

DisasterSociety addresses this question through the architecture developed in
\cref{sec:method}. An \emph{EventPack} and scenario adapter organize external
hazard, warning, population, road, resource, and provenance inputs. The
event-driven kernel exposes each resident only to delivery- and
location-appropriate information; resident adapters maintain private memory,
feedback, and plans; the interaction layer advances messages through explicit
life-cycle states; the household layer validates and versions coordination
commitments; and the world batches all submitted intentions before committing
physical change. Crucially, the language model is used inside the resident
cognition boundary, not as the source of physical truth or the resolver of shared
resources. The same boundaries expose mechanism switches, state snapshots,
message and commitment histories, execution reasons, model-call metadata, and
terminal run status for evaluation and audit.

This paper makes three contributions. First, a \emph{state and execution
architecture} for generative disaster social simulation that models
resident-private cognition, household-shared constraints and commitments, and
authoritative physical state as three separate state owners connected by
explicit transitions, rather than as one shared generative context
(\cref{sec:method}). Second, a \emph{closed social-process loop} linking the
two chains that connect talk to action: a social-coordination chain
(sent$\,\to\,$delivered$\,\to\,$processed$\,\to\,$accepted$\,\to\,$feasible
commitment) and an action chain (intention$\,\to\,$batched world
arbitration$\,\to\,$executed or rejected$\,\to\,$feedback to cognition), so
that the language model proposes but never directly mutates shared or physical
state (\cref{sec:dynamic-loop}). Third, \emph{mechanism-oriented experimental
evidence} that separates whether a resident mechanism acts at its own
interface from whether it changes the collective outcome
(\cref{sec:experiments}): in a paired twelve-seed ablation, memory, feedback,
planning, and interaction each pass their interface-level manipulation checks,
yet only feedback and interaction carry determined downstream
effects---removing feedback leaves residents acting on outdated private
beliefs after route closure and collapses complete safe household departure
from 0.812 to 0.021, while removing interaction eliminates coordinated
departure even though half of decision-capable residents still evacuate
individually. A deterministic in-kernel reference policy, a bounded
seven-model replication, and an instruction-wording sensitivity check
corroborate the findings from three further angles, and a claim--evidence
evaluation protocol keeps each claim tied to its appropriate evidence
\cite{sargent2013verification,klugl2008validation,grimm2005pattern}
(\cref{sec:evaluation-method}). The case is an empirical test bed operated
within explicit claim boundaries; it is not presented as a historical
reconstruction, an automatic city generator, or a population-level evacuation
predictor.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. \Cref{sec:related}
reviews protective-action and evacuation modeling, agent-based disaster
simulation, and generative-agent social simulation, and then synthesizes the
remaining systems gap. \Cref{sec:method} presents the
framework contracts, kernel, and resident adapters. \Cref{sec:experiments}
defines the shared experimental setup and reports the studies.
\Cref{sec:discussion} states interpretation boundaries and threats to
validity, and \cref{sec:conclusion} concludes.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Protective Action and Household Evacuation Behavior}

Protective-action research treats disaster response as a staged process: cues
and warnings must be received, attended to, and understood before threat and
action appraisal, which remain subject to situational impediments
\cite{lindell2012padm,mileti1992warnings,kuligowski2021wildfire}. Warning
exposure, preparation, and departure unfold over time rather than collapsing
into a single binary outcome \cite{grajdura2021awareness}; crisis-informatics
fieldwork shows that official messages reach residents unevenly and are
reinterpreted through social channels before being acted on
\cite{palen2018crisis}; and multiple information sources jointly drive risk
perception and evacuation dynamics \cite{raihan2022information}. The process is
also social at the household level: members distributed across locations
coordinate gathering and shared-vehicle use, and observed plans do not always
place all members in one departure group
\cite{huang2012ike,liu2014uniting,maghelal2017split}. This literature specifies
which social processes a resident-level model must represent; it does not
dictate a software state machine or establish that an LLM reproduces those
processes.

\subsection{Agent-Based Disaster and Evacuation Simulation}

Classical evacuation modeling is strong where generative cognition is weak.
Dynamic traffic models represent departure-time choice, route choice, and
network assignment at varying behavioral detail
\cite{pel2012travel,murraytuite2013overview}, and synthetic-population
pipelines match joint household and person attribute distributions so that
simulated cohorts remain demographically coherent
\cite{beckman1996synthetic,ye2009ipu,pritchard2012population,guo2007population}.
Belief--desire--intention evacuation models separate belief, intention, and
execution in software, but the resident decision unit is rule-based rather than
generative, and household commitments are not modeled as versioned shared
objects \cite{dawson2012warning}. This literature also supplies the evaluation
standards we adopt: verification and validation as distinct activities
\cite{sargent2013verification,klugl2008validation}, pattern-oriented modeling
\cite{grimm2005pattern}, systematic sensitivity analysis
\cite{tenbroeke2016sensitivity}, and transparent documentation
\cite{lorscheid2012blackbox,grimm2020odd}. DisasterSociety is upstream of
network simulators: it produces the departure parties and route intentions a
traffic model would then execute, and does not replace them.

\subsection{Generative-Agent Social Simulation}

Generative Agents combined episodic memory, reflection, planning, and
communication in a persistent artificial community
\cite{park2023generativeagents}, extending populated prototypes for social
computing \cite{park2022socialsimulacra}; surveys now catalog a fast-growing
design space of LLM-based autonomous agents \cite{wang2024surveyagents}, and
interactive benchmarks probe their social competence \cite{zhou2024sotopia}.
Subsequent platforms scaled LLM-agent simulation to societal and urban
settings, in some cases through hybrid LLM/numerical designs to control token
cost \cite{zhang2025agentsociety,bougie2025citysim,zhou2026gasim}. GPLab
couples generative agents with policy subsystems for policy simulation and
evaluation \cite{zhang2026gplab}, and Bail argues generative AI can augment
social-science measurement and simulation alike \cite{bail2024generativeai}. In the systems
reviewed here, agent communication is often represented without a separately
validated household-level commitment state; an authoritative physical world
that arbitrates whether a linguistically formed plan can execute is rare, and
mechanism-linked ablation of resident cognition is rarer still.

\subsection{LLM Agents in Disaster Response}

LLMs have entered the disaster pipeline at several points
\cite{lei2025disasterllmsurvey}. FLARE uses behavioral theory and LLM reasoning
for individual wildfire evacuation prediction on post-event surveys
\cite{chen2025flare}; Dang et al. embed LLM agents in a cellular-automata fire
environment \cite{dang2025fireagents}; Yang et al. couple LLM decisions with
pedestrian and vehicle evacuation in a dynamic ABM \cite{yang2026whenagents};
RESPOND joins flood forecasting, alerts, road closures, and population agents
in an integrated demonstration \cite{sultimov2026respond}, with a
coordination-focused variant adding episodic memory and cognitive task analysis
\cite{sultimov2026aamas}. Adjacent benchmarks probe geospatial reasoning in
emergency operations \cite{wang2026canllm} and multi-agent collaboration in
wildfire response \cite{hyun2025crew}, while design studies ask what makes such
simulations useful for policy practice
\cite{li2025whatmakes,li2026whatif,calzolari2026personalities}, and
recovery-oriented work couples generative agents with socio-technical regional
models \cite{blagojevic2026sociotechnical,cui2026llmresilient}. Two recent
works sharpen the temporal and cognitive organization of disaster response
itself: hierarchical generative agents organize one resident's sequential
evacuation decisions into three cognition levels and calibrate against
empirical human evacuation data \cite{mendoza2026hierarchical}, and city-scale
crisis-response simulation pushes the temporal realism of LLM-agent decisions
\cite{zhang2026temporal}. These works organize cognition \emph{within} a
resident; our focus is the complementary boundary \emph{between} residents and
the world---how private cognitions become household-level coordinated and
physically executable behavior.

Across these lines of work, the unresolved systems question is how to preserve
resident-private information, turn accepted messages into versioned household
commitments, and arbitrate physical execution against one authoritative world
state. DisasterSociety makes these boundaries explicit and evaluates their
mechanisms through controlled ablation (\cref{sec:method,sec:experiments}).


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

DisasterSociety is a discrete-time, event-driven platform for studying how
resident-level cognition and communication produce household and collective
outcomes under a changing disaster environment. The method is organized around
contracts rather than a monolithic ``LLM simulator.'' A resident model may
interpret information and return a typed intention but cannot directly mutate
shared resources or physical state. Interaction and household layers validate
responses and maintain versioned coordination records under adapter-specific
checks. Deterministic household and world code then allocate shared resources
and apply execution state; a stored commitment is therefore a validated
coordination record, not a guarantee of global feasibility before world
arbitration. This decomposition is documented consistently with ODD's emphasis
on explicit entities, state variables, process scheduling, observation, and
stochasticity \cite{grimm2020odd}. \Cref{fig:architecture} summarizes the
initialization inputs, the three persistent runtime states, the constrained
transition mechanisms that connect them, and their evolution to step $t+1$.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/fig2_method_framework.pdf}
  \caption{DisasterSociety method framework. Scenario initialization combines a
  time-indexed \emph{EventPack}, a household profile, and controlled case
  adaptation. The runtime maintains resident-private cognition $C_{i,t}$,
  household-shared state $H_{h,t}$, and authoritative world state $W_t$ as
  separate persistent states. Bounded observation converts accessible world and
  event context into resident-specific information $o_{i,t}$; resident
  deliberation produces a proposal or message $m_{i,t}$ and an action intention
  $I_{i,t}$; social coordination validates proposals against household
  constraints to form a versioned commitment $K_t$; and world arbitration
  resolves $I_{i,t}$ and $K_t$ against $W_t$, yielding an executed or rejected
  result. The resulting cognition, household, and world updates become
  $C_{i,t+1}$, $H_{h,t+1}$, and $W_{t+1}$ for the next step.}
  \label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{State Model and Ownership Boundaries}

Let residents be indexed by $i\in\mathcal{R}$ and households by
$h\in\mathcal{H}$, with a membership function $g(i)=h$. At simulation step $t$,
the state is
\begin{equation}
  S_t=\left(W_t,\{C_{i,t}\}_{i\in\mathcal{R}},
  \{H_{h,t}\}_{h\in\mathcal{H}},Q_t\right),
  \label{eq:state}
\end{equation}
where $W_t$ is authoritative world state, $C_{i,t}$ is resident $i$'s private
cognitive state, $H_{h,t}$ contains shared household constraints and accepted
coordination state, and $Q_t$ contains events and adapter-specific message,
receipt, and ledger state. These are separately owned namespaces. Updating
$C_{i,t}$ does not imply an update to $C_{j,t}$, and neither a private plan nor a
private belief becomes household or world state without an explicit protocol
transition. Thus, two co-resident adults may hold different warnings or route
beliefs; an unexpressed private plan is not a household agreement; and a
resident's belief that a road is open cannot reopen it in the physical world.

An access function $\Omega_i$ maps world state and delivery records to the
resident's bounded observation,
\begin{equation}
  O_{i,t}=\Omega_i\!\left(W_t,Q_{\leq t};A_{i,t}\right),
  \label{eq:observation}
\end{equation}
where $A_{i,t}$ encodes channel, recipient, location, and adapter-specific
access rules. Resident $i$ returns a typed intention
\begin{equation}
  I_{i,t}=\Pi_i\!\left(C_{i,t},O_{i,t};\theta,\phi\right),
  \label{eq:intention}
\end{equation}
where $\theta$ denotes the declared model backend and $\phi$ the enabled
mechanisms and prompt protocol. $I_{i,t}$ is a proposal, not a state mutation.
An interaction transition then processes the current inbox and returned message
responses into intermediate private and coordination state
\begin{equation}
  \left(\widetilde C_t,\widetilde H_t,\widetilde Q_t\right)=
  \mathcal{P}\!\left(C_t,H_t,Q_t,\{I_{i,t}\}_{i\in\mathcal{R}}\right),
  \label{eq:interaction-transition}
\end{equation}
and the world resolves all contemporaneous intentions against one physical
snapshot and the intermediate coordination state,
\begin{equation}
  \{R_{i,t}\}=\mathcal{A}\!\left(W_t,\{\widetilde H_{h,t}\},
  \{I_{i,t}\}\right),
  \label{eq:arbitration}
\end{equation}
and returns a keyed execution outcome $R_{i,t}$ containing status, executed
action, rejection reason, resource allocations, and state delta. Only keyed
outcomes can commit execution-derived resident fields or physical movement;
other cognitive and coordination transitions occur through delivery,
observation, and interaction processing.

The ownership boundaries in \cref{fig:architecture} map to four implementation
roles with distinct ownership. A \emph{Resident} is a cognitive and decision
unit. A
\emph{Household} is a system object that owns membership, vehicles, care
requirements, and accepted commitments; it is not a household-level LLM. The
\emph{Interaction} layer owns message state. The \emph{World} owns authoritative
hazard, route, location, and movement arbitration. Neither household nor world
state is writable by the resident model. Not every resident issues a
language-model decision at every step: attributes distinguish
\texttt{decision\_capable}, a persistent eligibility property, and
\texttt{decision\_policy}, which may be generative, rule-based, or dependent,
and a step-specific wake gate solicits a decision only when the resident has
pending inbox items, recent execution feedback, a due household commitment, an
incomplete plan, or a sufficiently close hazard. A dependent member can retain
location, safety, caregiver, and transit state without making a language-model
call, and a resident that has reached the safe zone with no open commitment or
plan is no longer woken.

\subsection{Information-Bounded Cognition and Structured Intentions}

All resident adapters obey an information boundary even though their JSON
payloads differ. A decision may include a profile, household facts visible to
that member, received inbox items, privately perceived hazard or routes,
selected private history, accepted commitments visible to the resident, and
recent execution outcomes. It never receives authority to edit the world.

The model returns a typed decision containing an assessment, an action from a
closed action set, optional destination, route, vehicle, and departure step,
accompanying dependents, outgoing messages, responses to received message IDs,
and optional memory writes. Schema validation constrains enums, field types,
and required structure, and identifier validity is enforced separately against
the resident's visible namespace; malformed output receives one schema-guided
repair attempt before the gateway records a logical failure.


\subsection{Social Coordination, Commitments, and World Arbitration}
\label{sec:messages-arbitration}

Messages carry sender, recipient or audience, channel, content, kind, structured
payload, sending step, and lifecycle metadata. The guaranteed lifecycle prefix
is \textit{sent}~$\rightarrow$~\textit{delivered}~$\rightarrow$~\textit{processed};
only an explicit recipient response may further move a processed receipt to
\textit{accepted} or \textit{rejected}. The states are not rhetorical labels:
delivery determines inbox membership, and processing controls when the message
enters private history; in particular,
$\textit{sent}\not\Rightarrow\textit{delivered}$ and
$\textit{accepted words}\not\Rightarrow\textit{executable action}$. Receipt
granularity is adapter-specific
(\cref{tab:adapter-contracts}); enqueueing alone never updates recipient memory.

An accepted proposal does not become shared coordination state until the
household layer validates it. Formally, a commitment $p$ is
\emph{household-valid} for household $h$ at step $t$ only when
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
  \Phi(p,h,t)=\mathbf{1}\big[\,&M(p)\subseteq D_h\;\land\;v(p)\in V_h\\
  &\land\;r(p)\neq\emptyset\;\land\;\tau(p)\geq t\,\big],
\end{split}
  \label{eq:commitment-feasibility}
\end{equation}
where $M(p)$ is the set of decision members named by the commitment, $D_h$ the
household's decision-capable members, $v(p)$ the proposed vehicle, $V_h$ the
household's vehicles, $r(p)$ the route, and $\tau(p)$ the departure step.
Household validity does not imply physical executability: route openness,
movement compatibility, dependent care, and vehicle capacity at departure
remain world-arbitrated at execution. When a newer validated
commitment revises an existing one, the prior version is cancelled but retained
with a supersession link. The shared household commitment is authoritative;
private member views need not be automatically synchronized, so message,
commitment, and execution records form separate funnels rather than one
relabeled count.

For each batch, the world converts intentions into resolved actions and checks
them against the physical snapshot: route openness and capacity, household
vehicle state, dependent accompaniment, and a compatible household commitment
where required. Contending vehicle requests are resolved using stable request
attributes and a named random stream only as the final tie-break, so experiment
condition labels cannot change an exogenous draw.

A keyed execution outcome is the only bridge from physical arbitration to
execution-derived resident fields and physical movement. Commitment validation
can reject a proposal for member, caregiver, vehicle, time, or overlap reasons,
and world arbitration can separately reject an executable intention for a
closed route, route capacity, vehicle conflict, future departure, missing
traveler intention, or dependent-accompaniment failure. When feedback is
enabled, recent rejection reasons and the current plan's
\texttt{last\_failure} become bounded input to a later decision, allowing the
resident to revise its intention without granting the LLM direct world access.
The resulting loop resembles reasoning with action feedback
\cite{yao2023react,shinn2023reflexion}, but checked feasibility comes from
deterministic validators rather than self-correction language alone.


\subsection{Event-Driven Process Loop and Causal Step Order}
\label{sec:dynamic-loop}

The kernel executes each step in six ordered phases, followed by run-level
terminal classification. \Cref{fig:dynamic-loop} follows the two runtime paths
that cross these phases: proposals move through recipient-specific message
states into household coordination, while action intentions are batched for
world arbitration and returned as execution feedback. (i)~\emph{World advance}:
the clock, hazard, and due exogenous events update the authoritative state, and
official warning or order receipts are created. (ii)~\emph{Wake}: the gate
selects residents to solicit. (iii)~\emph{Decision}: selected residents decide
concurrently against one immutable physical snapshot. (iv)~\emph{Interaction}:
the current inbox and returned message responses are processed, potentially
updating private memory and household coordination state.
(v)~\emph{Arbitration}: all decisions are wrapped, the complete physical-action
batch is resolved against one world snapshot, keyed execution outcomes are
committed, and outgoing messages are emitted. (vi)~\emph{Delivery and record}:
message delivery advances and the step snapshot is recorded. After the loop or
an exception, the run is classified as \textsc{valid}, \textsc{invalid}, or
\textsc{aborted} under the frozen operating protocol.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../figures/fig3_runtime_lifecycle.pdf}
  \caption{Runtime lifecycle of social coordination, arbitration, and feedback
  within step $t$. Resident cognition produces a proposal or message $m_{i,t}$
  and an action intention $I_{i,t}$. Messages move through sent, delivered, and
  processed states before social coordination either rejects the proposal or
  forms household commitment $K_t$. In parallel, collected intentions,
  commitment $K_t$, and authoritative state $W_t$ enter world arbitration,
  which returns executed or rejected outcomes. Read dependencies and outcome
  feedback produce $C_{i,t+1}$, $H_{h,t+1}$, and $W_{t+1}$ for the next step.}
  \label{fig:dynamic-loop}
\end{figure}

The order prevents several common causal errors. A resident cannot act on a raw
warning it did not receive, because delivery precedes waking and the prompt
reads the private inbox rather than the event list. All decisions observe the
same physical phase, typed outcomes precede execution-derived resident updates,
and rejected movement never consumes or mutates the corresponding physical
resource.

Message timing and receipt granularity are adapter-specific within this common
schedule. Household direct messages and community messages use separate
delivery models; both classes advance at step end and are processed when a
recipient next wakes. Enqueueing alone never updates recipient memory.


\subsection{Scenario and Population Instantiation}

An \emph{EventPack} is a provenance-aware scenario data package: a validated
manifest with named data assets and a provenance record. A scenario adapter
converts those assets, or a separately frozen experimental configuration, into
the \texttt{EventSource} contract consumed by
the kernel, which returns the exogenous events due at step $t$; warnings,
orders, hazard updates, and road events are never generated by resident
language models.

The Carr-informed case is deliberately a controlled scenario. Its final fire
perimeter is a single endpoint rather than a hazard time series; its two
routes are derived experimental assets; and the road disruption is a controlled
perturbation rather than an observed closure, with the sourced perimeter
retained as case provenance. The world may therefore hold hazard distance,
route state and capacity, resident and dependent locations, vehicle state,
care obligations, and household commitments.

The population pipeline starts from weighted household microdata, using
iterative proportional fitting (IPF) to adjust donor weights within tract
against household margins \cite{beckman1996synthetic,ye2009ipu}. Sampling is
atomic at the donor-household level: when a donor is selected, its member
records are copied together and receive new synthetic identifiers, preserving
internally observed relationships such as shared vehicles, co-residence, and
care dependencies. Person-level distributions are reported as diagnostics
alongside the fitted household margins: a plausible household structure, a
calibrated person population, and validated resident behavior are separate
claims.

Initialization converts each sampled household into member, resource, and care
state. Age 18 or older defines the transparent decision-capable baseline;
minors are dependent execution members, and care properties do not by
themselves remove an adult's decision capability. Vehicles carry household
ownership, location, capacity, occupancy, and reservation state.


\subsection{Two Experimental Operationalizations}
\label{sec:adapters}

The shared kernel hosts different operationalizations of resident cognition,
and the Carr case uses two adapters with deliberately different estimands
(\cref{tab:adapter-contracts}): one platform, two operationalizations, two
research questions. Both obey the same intention--outcome boundary and state
separation; they differ in cohort construction, private-state representation,
and the claims their artifacts support. The empirical-process operationalization
does not validate the controlled one, and the controlled one does not attempt
Carr behavioral reproduction; they share only the state ownership,
information boundary, and arbitration contracts above.

\paragraph{Empirical-process adapter.}
This adapter studies a Carr-informed resident process under a full mechanism
configuration with richer donor-microdata trait profiles. Residents are exposed
to a modeled communication substrate (household cliques, nearby neighbors, and
a friend ring---not observed Carr social ties), and coordination takes the form
of structured departure parties whose member, caregiver, vehicle, time, and
overlap checks run at acceptance, with route openness enforced by the world at
execution. A valid solo-adult intention may also form a solo party without
prior negotiation, so the adapter observes coordination where it occurs without
forcing every departure through dialogue; solo parties are reported separately
from multi-adult coordinated departures.

\paragraph{Controlled-mechanism adapter.}
This adapter supports paired mechanism identification: conditions share one
household cohort, an initial private primary-route plan, a time-indexed
disruption, and component-isolated exogenous streams, while four code-level
flags enable or remove memory, feedback, planning, and interaction. Its
defining feature for the ablation is the separation of private route perception
from world route truth: with feedback enabled, a decision copies changed world
route state into the private view and rejected execution adds bounded failure
feedback; with feedback disabled, the pre-closure view persists while the world
still arbitrates the true closure. Memory has a deliberately narrow operational
meaning here---delivery-gated warning retention plus model-emitted memory
writes---while the platform's general ranked memory
\cite{park2023generativeagents} remains available through the base resident
interface. The remaining contract differences are summarized in
\cref{tab:adapter-contracts}.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{Carr adapter contracts. Both adapters share the kernel, the typed
intention--outcome boundary, and household/world state separation, but differ
in cohort, private-state representation, communication record, and estimand.}
\label{tab:adapter-contracts}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{0.15\textwidth}Y Y@{}}
\toprule
Contract & Empirical-process adapter & Controlled-mechanism adapter \\
\midrule
Purpose and cohort & Carr-informed full-condition process study with richer
PUMS/Carr trait profiles; profile ordering and formal cohort are frozen &
Mechanism identification with seed-selected households containing exactly two
adults, at least one dependent, and exactly one reported vehicle \\
Private cognition & Inbox, recent memory list, perceived routes, private
commitment view, and execution feedback; no persistent executable plan &
Mechanism flags, warning-history signal, feedback-gated route view, executable
plan and plan history, accepted commitments, and recent failure feedback \\
Communication & Recipient-specific official receipts; social messages use a
message-level record over a recipient list and a constructed social graph &
Recipient-specific receipts; household direct and community-audience delivery
are distinct, but the community audience is not a social-network topology \\
Coordination & Structured departure parties with care, vehicle, member, time,
and overlap checks, plus an allowed solo auto-party path & Versioned controlled
household proposals and commitments for the two decision members, with final
execution checks in the world \\
Estimand & Empirical reasonableness and process-constraint correctness;
social-message quantities reported at message level & Paired full-versus-drop-one
mechanism contrasts with manipulation checks and downstream outcomes \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table*}


\subsection{Reproducibility Infrastructure: Gateway, Seeding, and Run Records}
\label{sec:gateway}

Three properties of the execution infrastructure bear directly on the evidence
reported in \cref{sec:experiments}; the remaining engineering details are
omitted here. First, every language-model call passes through a single audited
gateway that enforces schema validation, one repair attempt, budgets, and
transient retry, and records status, tokens, cost, and latency per call; failed
calls either abort the run under the frozen protocol or enter a logged rule
fallback that remains subject to world arbitration, and the fallback share is
reported alongside unrecovered failures for every run. Second,
controlled-scenario non-LLM randomness is drawn from component-isolated keyed
streams that deliberately exclude the experiment condition label, so paired
conditions share exogenous draws and a condition switch cannot masquerade as a
mechanism effect. Third, each run writes step-level world, intention, outcome,
and interaction records, a terminal summary, and configuration and source
fingerprints, making every reported number recomputable from the persisted
artifacts (\cref{sec:evaluation-method}).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

Three studies instantiate the claim families of our evaluation methodology: an
empirical-process study under the full condition with the richer adapter,
extended by a two-seed scale demonstration at 100 households
(\cref{sec:exp-e1}), the central mechanism ablation with paired
full-versus-removal runs over four mechanism switches (\cref{sec:exp-e2}), and
a bounded cross-model replication of the two determined mechanism effects under
seven decision models, together with an equivalent-prompt sensitivity check on
the same effects (\cref{sec:exp-e3}). All three run the Carr-informed
controlled scenario of \cref{sec:adapters}; none is a historical reconstruction
of the Carr Fire, and no reported quantity is a population-level evacuation
prediction. Existing platforms do not expose matched mechanism controls; we
therefore use a deterministic in-kernel reference policy on the same scenario
(\cref{sec:exp-e2}), compare mechanism conditions within one platform, and
replicate the determined effects across decision-model families and sizes.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:evaluation-method}

Evaluation is organized along two orthogonal dimensions. \emph{Analysis scale}
identifies whether a quantity concerns an individual, a household or network,
a collective population, or system operation. \emph{Evidence type} identifies
whether a result verifies implementation and causal contracts, assesses
empirical or behavioral reasonableness, estimates a mechanism-specific removal
effect, or measures robustness and efficiency. This separation follows
established simulation methodology: conceptual-model validity, implementation
verification, data validity, and operational validity answer different
questions \cite{sargent2013verification}; ABM evaluation combines face,
calibration, sensitivity, and statistical evidence rather than conflating them
\cite{klugl2008validation}; and pattern-oriented modeling confronts bottom-up
mechanisms with multiple observed patterns at different levels instead of a
single aggregate target \cite{grimm2005pattern}. Generative social simulation
adds language-model and prompt dependence, motivating component ablations and
replication-risk checks \cite{park2023generativeagents,bail2024generativeai}.
The distinction prevents a deterministic replay from being presented as
behavioral validity, a calibrated warning margin from being reused as
independent validation, or a large run from being treated as proof of realism.
\Cref{tab:evidence-map} maps the claim families to their primary evidence.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Claim--evidence map used to design the evaluation. Scales and evidence
types are independent; the table gives the primary combination rather than a
requirement to populate every possible cell.}
\label{tab:evidence-map}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{0.20\textwidth}Y Y@{}}
\toprule
Claim family & Primary quantities and analysis scale & Evidence required and
interpretive boundary \\
\midrule
Process and software correctness & Event order, delivery and processing
lifecycle, intention--execution invariants, resource conservation, terminal
status, and replay at system and household scales & Executed tests, invariant
checks, logs, provenance, failure and fallback accounting; does not establish
human behavioral validity \\
Empirical reasonableness & Warning/channel exposure, departure timing,
household constraints and group differences at individual, household, and
collective scales & Independent or prospectively held-out targets where
available; otherwise explicitly bounded respondent-sample references,
constraint violations, and planned human or expert judgment; calibrated inputs
are not independent confirmation \\
Mechanism function & Module-specific manipulation checks and downstream
household or collective consequences & Paired full-versus-removal runs with
predeclared direction and opportunity-to-act checks; estimates a conditional
removal effect, not every module interaction \\
Research utility and robustness & Seed, model, prompt, scale, latency, token,
cost, and failure variation at run and system scales & Multiple independent
runs and declared sensitivity conditions; operational scale alone is not
realism \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table*}

The Carr case uses two non-interchangeable populations. Carr-R retains a
version-frozen, de-identified respondent table for bounded empirical references
and auxiliary development diagnostics. Carr-S contains synthetic households for
multi-step social-process experiments; its agents are not one-to-one replicas
of Carr-R respondents. A survey field is assigned in advance to runtime input,
calibration, evaluation-only, or exclusion, and the same field cannot be both a
run input and its evaluation target in one experiment. The empirical-process
study uses
train-designated, non-outcome-conditioned Carr trait blocks, but the same
survey also supplies some aggregate outcome and timing references; these
comparisons therefore assess Carr-informed reasonableness rather than
independent external validation, and the small non-evacuator group and the
absence of completed blinded human or expert review further bound
behavioral-validity claims. Both populations are derived from public or
de-identified sources: Carr-R is a version-frozen, de-identified survey
respondent table, and Carr-S households are synthesized from public-use
microdata with fitted tract margins (\cref{sec:method}). No identifiable
individual records are used at runtime, and scenario outputs are computational
artifacts rather than representations of real persons.

For controlled mechanism evaluation, each module has a runner-aligned
manipulation check. Memory uses conditional retention of a delivered official
warning. Feedback uses the share of residents with a pre-closure primary-route
plan who later record a post-closure alternate-route update. Planning uses the
household share that completes a post-closure alternate-route replan.
Interaction uses the household share that forms, after closure, an
all-decision-member commitment referencing the controlled alternate route, a
known household vehicle, and an integer departure step. These are
scenario-specific manipulation checks rather than general
psychological-validity measures. Downstream quantities include all-member safe
location, closed-route rejection, multi-adult co-departure, dependent safety or
left-behind rejection, and shared-resource conflict, each computed from
authoritative final state rather than intermediate summary labels, with
analytically distinct denominators (e.g., safe arrival over unique initialized
decision-capable residents; multi-adult coordinated departure only among
households with at least two decision-capable adults executing in the same
party). A mechanism cannot receive credit from a metric it has no opportunity
to affect.

The controlled contrast for module $m$ and paired seed $s$ is
\begin{equation}
  \Delta_{m,s}=Y_{s,\mathrm{full}}-
  Y_{s,\mathrm{full}-m}.
  \label{eq:ablation}
\end{equation}
The estimand is the conditional effect of removing $m$ while the other modules
remain present; it is not a full factorial decomposition and does not identify
all interactions. The smallest run-level observation is a complete
$\text{seed}\times\text{condition}$ run, not a resident, household, or
resident-step. Full and removal cells sharing a seed form one paired contrast;
independent replication for that contrast is across distinct seeds, not between
the two cells within a seed. Seed-level differences are summarized by their
mean and a standardized paired effect,
\begin{equation}
  \bar{\Delta}_m=\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{s\in S}\Delta_{m,s},
  \qquad
  d_z(m)=\frac{\bar{\Delta}_m}{\hat{\sigma}_{S}(\Delta_{m,s})},
  \label{eq:paired-summary}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\sigma}_{S}(\Delta_{m,s})$ is the sample standard deviation of the
seed-level differences and $S$ is the declared seed set. Uncertainty intervals
are paired-$t$ intervals over seed pairs, and sensitivity is reported through
per-seed direction shares and exact-zero shares rather than $p$-value
thresholds.

All model traffic passes through the audited gateway (\cref{sec:gateway}); each
run records the configuration, prompt version, decision-model identifier, seed,
and per-call status, and every reported quantity is recomputed from
authoritative final state. Under the predeclared accounting rule, a cell that
fails to reach its planned step count, terminates with a non-VALID status, or
records a fallback rate above 1\% is excluded from analysis. Every cell
reported below is VALID with zero failed calls and zero fallbacks.

\emph{Reproducibility.} Every cell archives its configuration, run summary,
mechanism metrics, and provenance record under SHA-256 fingerprints, and the
gateway ledger retains per-call status, cache, and fallback accounting. The
ablation study's publication audit, for example, covers 15 frozen artifact hashes across the 60
cells and records no missing, unexpected, or duplicated cells. All tables and
figures in this section are recomputed from the archived per-cell artifacts by
the paired-contrast pipeline rather than transcribed from intermediate prose
reports.

\subsection{Do Households Complete the Response Process End to End?}
\label{sec:exp-e1}

This study exercises the full condition with the empirical-process adapter
(\cref{sec:adapters}): 24 households carrying PUMS/Carr-informed trait
profiles, a constructed social graph, recipient-specific official receipts, and
structured departure parties over 25 steps. Three seeds (7201, 8301, 9401)
complete all planned steps with terminal status VALID. The decision model is
DeepSeek-V4-Flash; the gateway records 1,497 calls (917 completed, 580
served from cache) with no failed and no fallback calls.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{Empirical-process outcomes over 3 seeds (24 households, 25 steps).
Rates are seed means $\pm$ SD; funnel, receipts, and violations are totals over
the three runs.}
\label{tab:e1-process}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lc@{}}
\toprule
Quantity & Value \\
\midrule
Complete safe household departure rate & $0.514 \pm 0.024$ \\
Coordinated-departure household rate & $0.333 \pm 0.042$ \\
Split-departure household rate & $0.042$ \\
Messages sent $\rightarrow$ deliv.\ $\rightarrow$ processed &
$1214 \rightarrow 710 \rightarrow 665$ \\
Messages accepted / rejected & $467\,/\,73$ \\
Departure parties executed (per seed: 10 / 15 / 13) & $38$ \\
Commitments accepted / executed & $15\,/\,38$ \\
Order receipts per run (vol.\ / mand.) & $5\,/\,15$ \\
Care, vehicle, capacity, caregiver violations & $0$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\Cref{tab:e1-process} summarizes the run-level outcomes. Roughly half of the
households reach a complete safe departure ($0.514 \pm 0.024$ across seeds;
per-seed values 0.500, 0.542, 0.500), one third depart as a coordinated
multi-adult party, and split departures are rare (0.042). The message funnel
retains every life-cycle stage---1,214 sent, 710 delivered, 665 processed, and
467 accepted against 73 rejected---so the lossy channel, not a broadcast
assumption, shapes coordination. Execution is similarly clean: 38 departure
parties execute across the three runs (10, 15, and 13 per seed) with no party
rejected at execution time, and the commitment ledger records 15 accepted and
38 executed commitments with zero rejections and zero cancellations. Across all
runs, world arbitration records zero care left-behind, vehicle-conflict,
capacity, and caregiver violations: every executed departure party satisfied
the household and resource contracts of \cref{sec:method}.

\paragraph{Distribution-level comparison with the frozen survey reference}
\Cref{tab:e1-dist} places the simulated distributions next to the frozen
Carr-R respondent-sample reference under the estimand alignment of the
reference protocol. The order-to-departure delay is measured identically on
both sides---hours from the first delivered order, mandatory or voluntary, to
the household's first executed departure---and is pooled over the 114
departing order-recipient households across the three 24-household runs and
the two 100-household runs of the scale demonstration below. The simulated CDF
is directionally consistent but compressed: 0.570 of households depart
immediately and 0.868 within thirty minutes, against 0.446 at both grid
points in the survey ($n=186$), and the survey's long right tail (mean 7.1 h;
3.2\% beyond 48 h) has no simulated counterpart. Two structural reasons bound
the comparison: the 25-step scenario window caps observable delays at 12.5 h,
and the half-hour decision cadence resolves each order at the next decision
point, so sub-step hesitation and multi-day delays are outside the modeled
behavior space. Under the frozen channel contract, survey channel prevalence
is a multi-select estimand among warned respondents that may not be compared
with simulated first-heard shares or with any normalized distribution
distance; we therefore report only like-for-like prevalence among order
recipients. All 197 simulated recipient households receive the official direct
channel by cohort construction, and 0.939 also receive at least one
interpersonal (community or intra-household) message, against survey
interpersonal prevalence 0.372 (Wilson 95\% CI [0.312, 0.435]); the survey
splits official receipt across concrete channels (reverse-911 0.359, text
0.380), a taxonomy the scenario collapses into one direct channel. At the
outcome level, the simulated individual departure share is 0.375 over all
members and 0.458 within order-recipient households, well below the survey
evacuation proportion 0.888 (Wilson 95\% CI [0.849, 0.918], $n=330$): unwarned
simulated households rarely self-evacuate, and split parties leave members
behind, whereas the survey estimand is an individual self-report and the
non-evacuator reference group contains only 37 respondents. The study
therefore supports Carr-informed process reasonableness rather than behavioral
validation, and the level gap is reported rather than tuned away; its evidence
state is \textsc{complete\_valid} following the formal promotion audit
(expected-cell completeness, terminal states, call accounting, provenance, and
recomputed aggregates all pass).

